([PROBLEM 2: Memory Address Space and Bus Bandwidth Derivation]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Statement: An engineer evaluates two microprocessor architectures: System A with) Tj
T*
(a 32-bit address bus and 32-bit data bus; System B with a 64-bit address bus and 64-bit) Tj
T*
(data bus operating with dual-channel DDR4 RAM clocked at 3200 MT/s \(MegaTransfers/sec\).) Tj
T*
(Calculate: \(1\) Maximum physical RAM addressable by each system, and \(2\) Peak memory) Tj
T*
(bandwidth of System B.) Tj
T*
(Mathematical Derivation:) Tj
T*
(* 1. Maximum Addressable RAM Capacity:) Tj
T*
(- System A \(32-bit address bus\): Addressable locations = 2^32 = 4,294,967,296 bytes = 4) Tj
T*
(GB \(Gigabytes\).) Tj
T*
(- System B \(64-bit address bus\): Addressable locations = 2^64 = 1.844 x 10^19 bytes = 16) Tj
T*
(Exabytes \(EB\).) Tj
T*
(* 2. Peak Memory Bandwidth Calculation for System B:) Tj
T*
(- Data bus width per channel = 64 bits = 8 Bytes.) Tj
T*
(- Dual-channel total data bus width = 2 x 8 Bytes = 16 Bytes.) Tj
T*
(- Transfer rate = 3200 x 10^6 transfers per second.) Tj
T*
(- Peak Bandwidth = 16 Bytes x 3200 x 10^6 transfers/sec = 51,200 MB/s = 51.2 GB/sec.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
([PROBLEM 3: Multi-Level Cache Memory Performance Analysis]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Statement: A modern computing processor features a two-level cache hierarchy: L1) Tj
T*
(Cache with access time of 1 nanosecond \(ns\) and 92% hit rate; L2 Cache with access time) Tj
T*
(of 5 ns and 98% local hit rate; Main Memory \(RAM\) with access time of 50 ns. Calculate) Tj
T*
(the Effective Memory Access Time \(EMAT\).) Tj
T*
(Mathematical Derivation:) Tj
T*
(* 1. Given Parameters: t_L1 = 1 ns, H1 = 0.92, Miss Rate 1 \(M1\) = 1 - 0.92 = 0.08.) Tj
T*
(* 2. L2 Parameters: t_L2 = 5 ns, Local Hit Rate H2 = 0.98, Local Miss Rate M2 = 1 -) Tj
T*
(0.98 = 0.02.) Tj
T*
(* 3. Main Memory: t_RAM = 50 ns.) Tj
T*
(* 4. EMAT Formulation:) Tj
T*
(- EMAT = t_L1 + M1 x \(t_L2 + M2 x t_RAM\)) Tj
T*
(- EMAT = 1 ns + 0.08 x \(5 ns + 0.02 x 50 ns\)) Tj
T*
(- EMAT = 1 ns + 0.08 x \(5 ns + 1.0 ns\) = 1 ns + 0.08 x 6.0 ns = 1 ns + 0.48 ns = 1.48) Tj
T*
(ns.) Tj
T*
(* 5. Engineering Conclusion: Despite main memory requiring 50 ns, the high-speed) Tj
T*
(multi-level cache reduces average access delay to just 1.48 ns \(a 33.7x performance) Tj
T*
(speedup\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
