/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
([PROBLEM 4: Architectural Case Study: Von Neumann vs Modified Harvard]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Context: Analyze why modern x86-64 and ARM processors are classified as Modified) Tj
T*
(Harvard architectures rather than pure Von Neumann systems.) Tj
T*
(In-Depth Architectural Analysis:) Tj
T*
(* 1. External Memory Level \(Von Neumann Aspect\): From the operating system and) Tj
T*
(programmer perspective, main memory \(DRAM\) is a single, unified flat address space) Tj
T*
(containing both executable binaries and user data. Both share external memory) Tj
T*
(channels.) Tj
T*
(* 2. Internal Microarchitectural Level \(Harvard Aspect\): Inside the CPU silicon die,) Tj
T*
(the L1 cache is strictly physically partitioned into two independent caches:) Tj
T*
(L1-Instruction Cache \(L1-I\) and L1-Data Cache \(L1-D\), driven by separate internal) Tj
T*
(buses.) Tj
T*
(* 3. Pipelining Benefits: The instruction fetch stage reads from L1-I simultaneously) Tj
T*
(while the memory load/store execution stage reads/writes to L1-D in the exact same) Tj
T*
(clock cycle without structural hazards or bus contention.) Tj
T*
(* 4. Hardware Realization: This hybrid design eliminates the Von Neumann bottleneck) Tj
T*
(in the high-frequency pipeline while retaining the software programming simplicity of) Tj
T*
(a single unified address space.) Tj
T*
